WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
Today we will be showing you how to enable
to Google Cloud Debugger on Google compute

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.910
engine the debugger is enabled by
default for App Engine and standard

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:14.049
manage VM images for Java the debugger
provides insight without requiring you

00:00:14.049 --> 00:00:18.289
to make any changes to your code. with
each snapshot it because possible to

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:22.050
view the call stack and variables of
your application

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:29.550
all without stopping or slowing down. 
All new projects have these API enables by

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:35.399
default. Older projects may need them
enabled. Connect with the cloud source

00:00:35.399 --> 00:00:44.489
repository using Gcloud beta source repos
clone default and the name of

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:53.829
your directory in this case sparky. Copy
the files to the new directory. And add

00:00:53.829 --> 00:00:59.780
the debugger agent to the startup script.
First download the latest version the

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:05.860
bootstrap script for cloud storage and
make it executable. Next, run the

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:10.860
bootstrap script giving it a relative
path to the application class files and

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.140
assigning the return java command line
arguments to the environment variable.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:22.390
lastly, we modify the java command line
to run the application – it's that simple

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:35.120
commit and push your source code to the
cloud source repository

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.740
and tell the debugger about source
context so that the Debugger can display

00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:44.120
the correct version of your deployed
application. Here we use GCloud preview

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:51.820
app gen-repo-info-file to output the source
context JSON to the class directory

00:01:51.820 --> 00:02:03.380
typically you'll do this during mavens
process resource phase. Build the project,

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:13.900
and deploy it

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:26.129
grab the IP address. Make sure works.
Navigate to source &gt; debug and to your

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:35.250
source file.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:39.580
So we can take a snapshot by clicking in
the gutter of line we wish to investigate.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:44.700
Now wait for an incoming requests that
causes the line to trigger the gathering

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:54.870
of the snapshot. That was fast! Look at the results
note that the app wasn't blocked and is

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.810
still running and handling incoming
traffic at full speed.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:08.760
you can also look at the call stack.
We've shown you how to enable and use

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.850
the debugger. For more information
take a look at the documentation. We

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:16.800
provide support on Stack Overflow and
the source of the examples on GitHub.

